    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(27, 17), (23, 88), (46, 6), (78, 3), (46, 97)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 39.0, 26.0, 29.0]\n    generator_capacities = [91.0, 73.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 300  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 500  # kV\n    line_capacity = 50  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 149090  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 39  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "This memo outlines the planning problem to design a transmission network that links our generation sites to load centers across a five-node footprint (two generators and three consumers). The nodes are positioned by their map coordinates in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (27, 17)\n  - N1 (generator): (23, 88)\n  - N2 (consumer): (46, 6)\n  - N3 (consumer): (78, 3)\n  - N4 (consumer): (46, 97), and their roles are defined in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Consumer requirements are provided in Consumer demands: N2: 39.0 MW, N3: 26.0 MW, N4: 29.0 MW, and generator output limits are given in Generator capacities: N0: 91.0 MW, N1: 73.0 MW.\n\nThe core decision is which transmission lines to build between nodes. Power can only flow along lines we construct, and each built line must respect a maximum transfer capability of 50 MW. Construction expense is distance-based; route lengths come from the node coordinates, and the per-kilometer build rate is $149,090/km.\n\nOperationally, we will use copper conductors, so resistive losses (heating) are the dominant variable cost. The wire material specification is provided in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the conductor size is specified in 300 mm\u00b2. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross-sectional area, so longer corridors and smaller conductors drive higher heating costs.\n\nThese resistive losses follow a square law: at a given voltage, doubling the power flow through a line produces roughly four times the heating. Physically, the lost power scales as I^2R; with current equal to power divided by voltage, the effect is proportional to the line resistance times (power divided by 500 kV) squared. We price this heating with the monetary rate in $39/MWh. In other words, the operational cost on a line is driven by the physics-based term\u2014line resistance times (power/voltage)^2\u2014multiplied by that rate. High voltage helps because it reduces current, so the loss cost falls with the square of voltage.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat power flow as conserved at the nodes: generators inject up to their limits, consumers must receive their demand, and we account for losses as a separate financial penalty rather than deducting energy from the delivered flow. It is not sufficient to simply sum flows or use linear approximations for losses; we must capture the square-law behavior to reflect how pushing more power through a corridor quickly becomes expensive.\n\nThe objective is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that all consumer demands are met, generator limits are respected, line capacities are not exceeded, and the total cost\u2014construction plus the quadratic heating costs\u2014is minimized. In practice, this means favoring configurations that shorten route lengths (to reduce resistance), provide adequate conductor size, and leverage our operating voltage to mitigate current, while balancing the distance-based build cost against the rapidly increasing resistive loss cost at higher flows.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Our utility needs to design a cost-effective transmission network connecting two generator sites to three consumer locations. We have five nodes placed on a geographic map; their locations are provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and their roles (generator or consumer) are defined in {NODE_TYPES}. The planning decision is which transmission lines to build between these nodes so that all consumer demand is reliably served at the lowest total cost.\n\nDemand at the consumer nodes must be fully supplied, as specified in {POWER_DEMANDS}. Generator outputs are limited by the capacities in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. Power can only flow along lines that we decide to construct, and each built line must respect a maximum transfer limit of {LINE_CAPACITY}. For planning purposes, power flow is treated as conserved at each node: generator outputs and line flows must exactly match consumer withdrawals. Resistive losses are accounted as a separate financial penalty and are not deducted from the nodal balance.\n\nCapital cost is driven primarily by the length of the corridors we build. Using the map distances between nodes, construction cost is proportional to route length at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. Shorter lines reduce capital outlay, but we must also consider operating costs arising from resistive heating.\n\nAll lines use copper conductors. The material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the selected wire cross-sectional area is {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. For any candidate line, resistance increases linearly with length and decreases with cross-section, so longer, thinner lines are more lossy than shorter, thicker ones.\n\nResistive losses follow a square law with respect to power flow. In physical terms, heating scales with the square of current (I^2R), and current is proportional to power divided by voltage. Consequently, the operational cost on a line is determined by the physics-based relationship: {LOSS_COST_RATE} multiplied by the line\u2019s resistance and the square of the power flow divided by {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. Doubling the power flow on a given corridor results in four times the resistive losses and four times the associated heating cost. This model explicitly captures the 1/V^2 mitigation effect: higher voltage reduces current for the same power transfer, which materially lowers loss-related costs.\n\nThe design decision is therefore a trade-off: building more or longer lines increases capital expense but can spread flows and keep the quadratic heating costs in check; concentrating power on fewer lines saves construction cost yet risks significantly higher loss costs because the square law punishes high flows. The optimum network minimizes the combined total of construction costs and these physics-based operational costs, subject to respecting generator limits, line capacities, and the requirement that every consumer node receives its full demand.\n\nPractically, we will evaluate candidate connections using their geographic separation, apply copper resistance based on length and cross-section, and compute the resulting loss cost using the square-law model above. We will select the set of lines to build that achieves the required deliveries with the lowest total cost, recognizing that losses are accounted financially and not deducted from delivered power."
